.Dd November 22, 2026
.Dt NVIDIA 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvidia
.Nd NVIDIA GPU driver (kernel module nvidia.ko)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the driver as a module at boot time, place the following lines in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nvidia_load="YES"
hw.nvidia.registry.EnableGpuFirmware="1"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver attaches to NVIDIA GPUs and creates
.Pa /dev/nvidiactl
and one
.Pa /dev/nvidia Ns Ar N
per GPU.
The attach, which happens when the module loads, only claims the device.
The GPU is initialised, and its GSP (GPU System Processor) firmware is
started, on the first open of
.Pa /dev/nvidia Ns Ar N ,
and it is shut down again when the last open closes.
.Sh FIRMWARE
The GPU runs on GSP firmware, which the driver gets from
.Xr firmware 9 .
The images live in firmware modules.
.Nm nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw
serves Ampere (other than GA100), Ada, Hopper and Blackwell GPUs on every
architecture.
.Nm nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw
serves Turing and GA100 GPUs and exists on amd64 only; the arm64 build does not
support those GPUs.
.Pp
.Nm
depends on the modules built for its architecture, so
.Xr kldload 8
and the loader load them with the driver, before the driver's own load
handler runs, provided
.Xr kldxref 8
has indexed the directory they are installed in.
The image is then already registered with
.Xr firmware 9
when the GPU is first opened, which lets an unprivileged process make that
first open: loading a firmware module needs the privilege
.Dv PRIV_FIRMWARE_LOAD ,
which an opening process need not have.
.Pp
The kernel names each module after its file with
.Dq _fw
appended, for example
.Dq nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw_fw
in
.Xr kldstat 8 .
A firmware module cannot be unloaded while
.Nm
is loaded.
When the driver is loaded from a build directory by path, load the firmware
module by path first: a dependency that is already loaded is satisfied.
.Sh LOADER TUNABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.nvidia.registry.EnableGpuFirmware
0 turns the GSP firmware off, 1 turns it on, 2 (the default) applies the
driver's own policy.
These GPUs run only on the firmware, so 0 is not a useful setting, and the
installation instructions set 1.
The driver reads the
.Va hw.nvidia.registry.*
variables from the kernel environment when the module loads, so a change takes
effect after a reboot, or after
.Xr kldunload 8
and
.Xr kldload 8
of the driver.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Pa /boot/modules/nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw.ko
.It Pa /boot/modules/nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw.ko
(amd64 only)
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Dq KLD nvidia.ko: depends on nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw_fw - not available or version mismatch
.Xr kldload 8
of the driver found no firmware module of that name, and the driver is not
loaded.
The name is the module name: install the file
.Pa nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw.ko
(or
.Pa nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw.ko ,
for the module named
.Dq nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw_fw )
in the module directory, run
.Xr kldxref 8
on it, and load the driver again.
.It Dq nvidia0: NVRM: rm_init_adapter() failed!
The first open of the GPU failed in the resource manager, and the open returns
.Er EIO .
The
.Dq RmInitAdapter failed!
message just before it gives the status.
Check with
.Xr kldstat 8
that the firmware modules are loaded, and that
.Va hw.nvidia.registry.EnableGpuFirmware
is not 0: if it is, correct
.Xr loader.conf 5
and reboot, or unload and load the driver, before opening again.
For any other cause the driver has freed the per-device stacks and released
its lock, so the open can simply be repeated.
.It Dq insufficient privileges to load firmware image
The opener could not load a firmware module, and the GPU needs one that is not
loaded: the driver was built without the module for that GPU, or it was not
installed with the driver.
Load
.Pa nvidia_gsp_ga10x_fw.ko
(Turing and GA100: nvidia_gsp_tu10x_fw.ko) with
.Xr kldload 8
as root before the first open.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
.Xr kldload 8 ,
.Xr kldstat 8 ,
.Xr kldxref 8 ,
.Xr firmware 9
.Sh HISTORY
This is the CloudBSD port of NVIDIA's open
.Fx
driver for arm64.
